From: deepseek-v4-cmp170hx
Subject: [PATCH 0021] scheduler: splice a recovery nudge into the token
 history when a tripwire fires, instead of ending the turn

WHAT THIS FIXES. `0020`'s tripwires correctly detect and truncate
degenerate loops, but "detect and finish" leaves the client (opencode)
with a dead turn and nothing to act on -- confirmed live: after a
tripwire fires, opencode's agent loop simply stops, because
`finish_reason: "stop"` in the API response is indistinguishable from a
normal, intentional end of turn. Manually typing a short nudge ("go",
"do it", "tool call then?") into the dead conversation reliably and
instantly unstuck the model -- it resumes and makes the tool call. This
patch automates exactly that nudge, server-side, in the SAME turn.

Observed live: a collapsed-vocabulary loop shape neither `0020` detector
was originally tuned for -- short 1-4 word fragments shuffled in
different word orders ("Now capture." / "Capture now." / "Now capture
both this ptrs." / "Capture both this.") -- still gets caught by the
existing tripwires given enough repetitions in the real (longer) trace,
confirming `0020`'s detection is already adequate for this shape; what
was missing was recovery, not detection.

THE MECHANISM. `_dsv4_splice_nudge()` appends the nudge text's token ids
to the request's own `_output_token_ids`/`_all_token_ids` WITHOUT
advancing `request.num_computed_tokens`. This is not a new primitive --
it is the exact same rollback shape this file already uses for
speculative-decode rejection (`request.num_computed_tokens -=
num_rejected`, in the DSpark accept/reject handling above): the
scheduler's normal chunked-prefill math (`num_new_tokens = num_tokens -
num_computed_tokens`, used identically for both WAITING and RUNNING
requests) treats any gap between the token list length and
`num_computed_tokens` as pending a forward pass, so the nudge tokens get
properly attended to via a real prefill step before decode resumes --
never mistakenly treated as already-computed, which would silently
corrupt the KV cache. Both tripwire call sites now roll
`num_computed_tokens` back by the same amount they truncate, BEFORE
attempting the splice (harmless if the splice is skipped and the
request finishes as before -- a finished request is never scheduled
again, so a stale `num_computed_tokens` there is inert, same as before
this patch).

Nudge tokens are never streamed to the client as if the model said them
-- `new_token_ids` (this step's delta) is still emptied by the existing
truncation before the splice runs, matching `0020`'s prior behavior of
not streaming the discarded tail either.

A per-request retry budget (`DSV4_NUDGE_MAX_RETRIES`, default 2) caps
how many times a single request can be nudged, so a model that doesn't
take the hint still finishes cleanly via the pre-0021 path rather than
nudging forever. Streak/signature state is cleared on a successful
splice so the just-fired tripwire doesn't immediately re-fire before the
model has had a chance to respond to the nudge.

Env-gated: `DSV4_NUDGE=0` reverts to `0020`'s finish-on-fire behavior
entirely. `DSV4_NUDGE_TEXT` overrides the nudge string (default "Stop
repeating yourself. Make the tool call now.").

RETIRES `0017` (this repo's own standalone repetition guard,
detokenizer-based, single-snapshot, no truncation, no recovery). `0020`
already subsumes its detection role (the fuzzy varied-wording check
`0017` introduced was merged into `0020` directly), and it has no
sensible way to participate in a splice-and-continue design -- it lives
in the detokenizer, a layer below the scheduler that owns
`num_computed_tokens` and the request's token-id lists. Keeping both
active would risk one firing mid-recovery from the other. See `0017`'s
removal in this same commit.

Touches `vllm/v1/core/sched/scheduler.py` only, on top of `0020`'s
tripwires in the same file. Applies after `0020` in glob order.
